{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST bundle a skill for deriving initial requirements from an existing codebase. The skill MUST direct agents to read the code and tests, map every first-party test to at least one requirement or example, identify and map every source unit implementing end-user-visible functionality or interactions to a requirement, and report gaps rather than claim complete coverage without an independent inventory review.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "Judged against the subject text, `requirements.cli.bootstrap_skill`. The requirement is documentation content: Habeas bundles a skill, and the skill must direct four things. The test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bootstrap_skill_is_bundled_and_directs_complete_inventories]` checks each of them. It reads the bundled skill text through `assert_phrases` and `assert_in_order`. Both helpers fail with a message naming the missing phrase. I read the real `SKILL.md` sections and confirmed the asserted phrases match them. I did not check the workflow-section phrase \"Follow behavior through the code\" or the 'Read the existing requirements…' phrase against the file, so those two are unconfirmed.\n\nMapping of obligations to assertions:\n- **Bundled:** `SKILLS.contains(&\"habeas-bootstrap-requirements\")` checks the skill is listed. `description(...)` must start with \"Use when adopting Habeas in an existing codebase\". `assert_phrases` reads the bundled skill through `bundled_skill`, so a missing skill would fail the test. `bundled_skills_are_five_skill_directories_each_home_to_its_topics` also checks the directory and front matter, but it is not a reference to this subject and isn't needed here.\n- **Read the code and tests:** the test asserts \"Read the existing requirements, documentation, public entry points, implementation, and tests\" and \"Follow behavior through the code\".\n- **Map every first-party test to a requirement or example:** the test asserts the Inventories text \"every first-party runnable test or test case\", \"Record the behavior each test checks; fill in requirement names once they are installed\", and the Tagging text \"Every test must end up with a `{test}` reference to a requirement or example\".\n- **Identify and map every end-user-visible source unit:** the test asserts \"every function, method, struct, enum, and union, listed from the source to discover behavior no requirement states\". It also asserts \"public APIs, commands, handlers, UI actions, configuration, outputs, errors, and distinct observable branches\" and \"A tagged entry point doesn't mean every observable branch beneath it has a requirement\". For the mapping step it asserts \"Give every inventoried test and each requirement's roots their references\". The Tagging paragraph also says to map other inventoried items to the requirement whose root reaches them. The test doesn't assert that sentence, but the asserted root-reference phrase covers the mapping obligation.\n- **Report gaps and don't claim complete coverage without an independent review:** `assert_in_order` checks, in sequence, \"recount both inventories from the source and tests without your lists\", the gap-list format line \"- <item or behavior> (<file>:<line>): <reason>; <open question>\", \"Claim 100% only when both inventories have no unmapped items\", and \"Don't silently exclude difficult cases\". The ordering assertion also checks the independent recount comes before the completeness claim.\n\nBecause the requirement is about what the skill text directs, asserting these exact phrases verifies every clause. A removal or weakening of any clause would fail the test. I found no unmapped obligation, wrong expected result, or missing assertion. No acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "a41a2920cceb608b61a78120cb3754621a2f77f6e7943141d0ef70b81bece077",
